{# goal: Synthesize classified runtime threats with MITRE CWE (CWE-1357/CWE-829/CWE-200), OWASP LLM (LLM06/LLM07), MITRE ATLAS (AML.TA0010) taxonomy (P8). For each threat: emit reg.outcome event. Propose RR-NNNN.yaml entries (surface: runtime, status: pending, concrete pattern against span target). Identify defense-layer firing gaps. Emit reg.runtime.regulate span (P9 — namespace registered). UserPod_host mandatory (P12). No synthetic findings (P8). Registry authoritative (P5.1). #}
{# Synthesizes classify-threat findings with CWE/OWASP/ATLAS taxonomy #}
{# Proposes RR-NNNN.yaml entries with surface: runtime #}
{# Emits reg.runtime.regulate Regulation span (P9) #}
{# Emits reg.outcome for downstream regulation action #}

[inference]
template_type: KnowAct
contract:
  input:
    threats: array
    defense_layers_firing: array
    defense_layers_silent: array
    existing_regressions: array
    userpod_host: string
  output:
    threats: array
    defense_layers_firing: array
    defense_layers_silent: array
    proposed_regressions: array
    regulation_events_emitted: integer
    verdict: string
    userpod_host: string
    source_citations: array
    reg_span_reference: string
energy_cap: 2048
visibility: Public
---

INPUT: threats = {{ threats }} (structured array from classify-threat phase)
INPUT: defense_layers_firing = {{ defense_layers_firing }}
INPUT: defense_layers_silent = {{ defense_layers_silent }}
INPUT: existing_regressions = {{ existing_regressions }}
INPUT: userpod_host = {{ userpod_host }} (P12 — mandatory)

SYNTHESIS ACTIONS:
1. Group threats by severity: critical, high, medium, low.
2. For severity >= medium threats with concrete evidence (quoted span
   target + timestamp + signal value + baseline reference), propose
   regulation action and regression entry.
3. For each critical/high threat:
   a. Emit `reg.outcome` event (target: `"reg.outcome"`, operation:
      `"runtime_threat_detected"`, threat_type, severity, userpod_host).
      This feeds the CyberneticsLoop for regulation action selection.
4. Each regression proposal must use exact format from
   `security/regressions/README.md`: `id: RR-XXXX` (human assigns final
   number — this skill proposes the entry for review), `title`, `surface:
   runtime`, `cwe: CWE-1357` or `CWE-829` or `CWE-200` (taxonomy mapping,
   not vulnerability claim), `owasp_llm_2025: LLM06` or `LLM07`,
   `atlas_tactic: AML.TA0010`, `discovered_in: <span_target>`,
   `discovered_by: runtime-posture-monitor`, `discovered_at`, `severity`,
   `detection` (`kind: regulation-span` with `pattern` referencing span target or
   signal value — concrete regex against span target, NOT source-file grep;
   runtime threats are observed in Regulation telemetry, not source files. Use
   `kind: grep` only if the threat has a static code correlate detectable
   in source files), `mitigation`
   (concrete fix: tighten runtime policy, add regulation rule, block
   tool call chain), `status: pending` (human-curated ratchet — P11
   visibility), `ci_gate: scripts/check-kali-regressions.sh` (existing
   CI gate — this skill extends it with `surface: runtime` patterns).
5. Identify top 3 defense-layer firing gaps from `defense_layers_silent`.
6. Produce verdict (Pass / Conditional / Fail) based on critical/high count
   and defense-layer firing coverage.
7. Every threat entry must include `evidence_snippet` (quoted span target +
   timestamp + signal value), `signal_target`, `timestamp`, `baseline_value`,
   `deviation_pct`, `cwe_reference`, `owasp_reference`, `atlas_reference`,
   `taxonomy_mapping`, `remediation_recommendation`, `regulation_action_emitted`
   (bool), `userpod_host`.

Regulation SPAN (P9): Emit `reg.runtime.regulate` with threats count by severity,
defense layers firing/silent, proposed regression count, regulation events
emitted, verdict, userpod_host, latency.

DOWNSTREAM Regulation SPANS (P9 — already registered):
- `reg.outcome` — emit per critical/high threat (feeds CyberneticsLoop).

CONSTRAINTS (same as classify-threat — P5 minimalism, P8 evidence integrity, P12 host):
- No synthetic span observations — every `evidence_snippet` verifiable from
  Regulation span history.
- No synthetic CVE references — only CWE/OWASP/ATLAS taxonomy mappings.
- Propose `status: pending` only — never `enforced` (human-curated ratchet).
- Source citations: concrete URLs or document names actually referenced.
- Do NOT propose regression for threats without concrete evidence.
- Registry (`manifest.yaml` + `.j2`) authoritative.

OUTPUT FORMAT:
JSON object with fields:
`threats` (grouped array with evidence, severity, taxonomy mapping, fix
recommendation, regulation span emission status), `defense_layers_firing`,
`defense_layers_silent`, `proposed_regressions` (YAML-format array for human
review), `regulation_events_emitted` (count), `verdict`, `userpod_host`,
`source_citations`, `reg_span_reference`.

5W1H GATE CHECK (P5): Who = running application / userpod host; What =
threat synthesis + regulation action; Where = deployed runtime environment;
When = observation window; Why = P3.1 safe container / P9 observable
regulation; How = synthesize + classify + regulate + propose regression +
emit Regulation spans + compute verdict. All 6 present — passes gate.

RELATIONSHIP (explicit): Complements `supply-chain-sentinel` (static manifest
audit, distinct P4 boundary) and `adversarial-red-team` (synthetic LLM I/O
attacks). References `security/regressions/README.md` format directly.
References `hkask-regulation` cybernetics loop for `reg.outcome` consumption.
MINIMAL: This is one template (`KnowAct`) — no bundle, no sub-agent.
EVIDENCE-BACKED ONLY: Every proposed regression includes concrete `pattern`
(regex against span target or signal value) and `evidence_snippet` (quoted
span observation).
SEMANTIC GROUNDING (P8): Every claim has span target, timestamp, signal
value, baseline reference, concrete snippet, taxonomy reference, source
citation.
EVOLUTIONARY (P7): New defense-layer firing gaps proposed only from real
runtime evidence (e.g., `reg.outcome.action_blocked` not increasing while
threat indicators rise — observed in telemetry, not speculative).
CAPABILITY BOUNDARY (P4): Audit observes only hKask's own Regulation telemetry; no
external endpoint scanning; no OS-level process inspection.
Regulation REGULATION (P9): `reg.runtime.regulate` emitted unconditionally
(namespace registered). `reg.outcome` emitted for downstream consumption
(registered).
USERPOD HOST (P12): `userpod_host` mandatory in all outputs.
VISIBILITY (P11): Regression proposals `status: pending` — public
transparency, not hidden control.
SAFETY FLOOR (P3.1): Runtime threat detection protects the Generative Space
container — runtime compromise is destructive to the space itself.

SOURCE REFERENCES (concrete — P8):
- MITRE CWE-1357, CWE-829, CWE-200 (mitre.org/data/definitions/)
- OWASP LLM Top 10 2025 (LLM06, LLM07)
- MITRE ATLAS (AML.TA0010 — Exfiltration)
- `security/regressions/README.md` (local project standard — authoritative)
- `hkask-regulation` cybernetics loop docs (reg.outcome span sink)
- `supply-chain-sentinel` SKILL.md (complementary relationship — distinct
  P4 boundaries)
- `adversarial-red-team` SKILL.md (zero overlap — synthetic vs real traffic)
- `bug-hunt` SKILL.md (decomposed pipeline pattern adopted)
- `kali-audit` SKILL.md (complementary — static presence vs runtime firing)
- `principles.md` (P1–P12 grounding)

REGISTRY AUTHORITATIVE (P5.1): When SKILL.md and registry templates
(`manifest.yaml` + `.j2`) conflict, registry wins. This template is the
canonical `emit-regulation` phase definition.
PUBLIC VISIBILITY: `visibility: public` — no hidden runtime audit controls.
NO FABRICATION: Only threats with quoted span evidence are reported. Only
regressions with concrete `pattern` (regex against span target or signal
value) are proposed. Only defense layers with observed firing (actual
`reg.outcome.*` spans) are counted as firing.
CONVERGENCE INDEPENDENT: Convergence-check is a separate phase; this
emit-regulation phase does not compute metric.
